#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Unsafe-code gate: the strictest settings each language offers stay in the files every build reads, and the
build under those settings is clean (plan steps P0.10.1, P0.10.2 and P0.10.3).

Usage: unsafe-code [--rust] [--tables-from <ref>] <repo>

With --rust only the Rust half runs and the Swift half says "not run"; that is for a runner with no Swift, and a
run that skipped a half never stands in for a pass of that half. With --tables-from the reviewed-path list
tools/gates/unsafe-allowed.txt is read from that commit of the repository (git cat-file of the blob at
<ref>:tools/gates/unsafe-allowed.txt, at most 256 KiB, UTF-8), so a branch cannot exempt a path by adding a line to
its own list; a ref that is not a plain commit name, a list over the cap, not text, or not a regular file at the ref
is broken, and a commit with no list means no reviewed path.

Rust (when the root Cargo.toml exists), settings first, each read as TOML:
  - the root [workspace.lints] denies unsafe_code and the Clippy lints panic, unwrap_used, expect_used,
    indexing_slicing, arithmetic_side_effects, disallowed_types and disallowed_methods; a lint that is missing or
    below deny is refused, and so is a group that holds one of them (warnings, clippy::all, clippy::style,
    clippy::restriction) set below deny;
  - [profile.dev] and [profile.release] set panic = "abort", and no profile sets panic = "unwind";
  - every workspace member has [lints] workspace = true, and a tracked Cargo.toml outside the workspace is refused;
  - the FFI crate crates/sb-engine-vt carries its own table, equal to the workspace table except unsafe_code;
  - the root clippy.toml disallows the std collections and std::sync::mpsc::channel, holds no test exemption
    (allow-unwrap-in-tests and the like) and no arithmetic allowance, and is the only clippy.toml in the tree;
  - no Cargo config file cargo reads from the repo upward, or from CARGO_HOME, sets rustflags that allow, warn or
    cap a lint, swaps the compiler, aliases clippy or sets a Clippy or rustc variable; nor does RUSTFLAGS,
    CARGO_ENCODED_RUSTFLAGS, CARGO_BUILD_RUSTFLAGS, CARGO_HOST_RUSTFLAGS or CARGO_TARGET_<triple>_RUSTFLAGS in the
    environment, and CLIPPY_CONF_DIR, RUSTC, CARGO_BUILD_RUSTC and the rustc wrapper variables (RUSTC_WRAPPER,
    RUSTC_WORKSPACE_WRAPPER and their CARGO_BUILD_ forms) are not set. A build script cannot lower a lint: cargo
    takes only -l and -L from its rustc-flags;
  - Clippy's old names for a denied lint (option_unwrap_used, integer_arithmetic and the like, from Clippy's own
    renamed list) and the checks that would hide one, renamed_and_removed_lints and unknown_lints, are not set
    below deny in the workspace table.
No lint from the deny list, an old name for one, a group that holds one, renamed_and_removed_lints or
unknown_lints is waived in source (allow, expect, warn, or any of them inside cfg_attr) outside a path in
tools/gates/unsafe-allowed.txt. The one built-in exception: files under
crates/sb-bounds/ may waive disallowed_types and disallowed_methods, and nothing else, because that crate wraps the
std collections on purpose. The waiver reader does not parse Rust, so an attribute written in a comment or string
counts too; reword it. The waiver reader reads only .rs files, so Rust source that pulls in code from another
file is refused outright outside a reviewed path: include! of any file, include_str! or include_bytes! of a .rs
file or of a path that is not one plain string, and a path attribute, plain or inside cfg_attr. That is the simplest
rule that fails closed: no file type can carry a waiver past the reader. When the settings hold, cargo clippy runs
over the whole workspace, all targets and all features with no lint flags of its own, so it reports what a plain
build with every feature on reports; any diagnostic is refused, in a file of any type. Limit: clippy runs for the
host target only, so code behind a cfg for another target (target_os, target_arch) is not linted here; a Mac
runner per target is the planned fix.

Swift: every tracked Package.swift is read through swift package dump-package, so only the evaluated manifest
counts. The tools version is 6.2 or higher; the package lists language modes of 6 or higher; every target has
.treatAllWarnings(as: .error) with no condition, no lowered warning, no unsafeFlags (the gate cannot read what a raw
flag does, so warnings as errors through one does not count) and no language mode below 6; EngineSurface also has
.strictMemorySafety(), and TerminalView, DesignSystem, FeaturePlaceholder and PlatformShell have
.defaultIsolation(MainActor.self) with no condition. Each setting is read from the evaluated manifest, so one inside
a comment, a string or an unused constant does not count. When the manifest holds, swift build runs with no flags of
its own. Swift files outside any package are type-checked in mode 6 with warnings as errors. Raw-pointer, bit-cast
and unmanaged APIs are refused by name outside a reviewed path: the Unsafe pointer types under any prefix
(AutoreleasingUnsafeMutablePointer too), OpaquePointer, withUnsafe calls, unsafeBitCast, unsafeDowncast, Unmanaged,
the unsafeAddress accessors and unowned(unsafe). Comments and string text are blanked before the names are read,
and code interpolated into a string still counts; a file with a block comment, multi-line string or interpolation
that never closes is broken.

Reviewed paths are listed in tools/gates/unsafe-allowed.txt, one per line, each with a "# reason"; an entry with no
reason, an absolute entry or one with ".." is broken. An entry names one file or, matched on whole path parts,
everything under a folder.

Files are listed and read through the repo-rules reader beside this file, with its caps: tracked regular files
only, a byte cap per file, an entry cap on the listing, and a symlink or special file breaks the run. Each build
runs under a time limit and an output cap; past either the check is broken. At most the first 1000 refusals are
printed and the rest are counted. A refusal names a file, line, setting or variable, never source text. Each run
first proves its own source and flag readers refuse their seeded controls. A missing compiler for code that exists
is broken, never a pass. Exit 0 clean, 1 refused, 2 broken.
Plan-Step: P0.21.5
Plan-Step: P0.10.1
Plan-Step: P0.10.2
Plan-Step: P0.10.3
"""
